,

    // Engram: local-only persistent memory (Gentleman-Programming/engram).
    // Installed via Nix-pinned Homebrew tap, gated to hosts listed in
    // modules/engram-hosts.nix -- see flake.nix + casks.nix for the tap/cask,
    // and homebrew.nix for the one-time `brew trust` activation reminder
    // (Homebrew's Tap Trust gate has no declarative/non-interactive bypass).
    //
    // This is Engram's documented "manual MCP-only" path, not its
    // `engram setup opencode` command. That command would imperatively copy a
    // plugin file into ~/.config/opencode/plugins/ and rewrite this very file
    // at runtime -- exactly the kind of out-of-band mutation this repo's
    // declarative-Nix model rules out. Writing the block below ourselves
    // gets the same MCP tool access with zero imperative steps.
    //
    // Deliberately NOT running `engram serve`: confirmed from Engram's own
    // docs that `engram mcp` (stdio) works standalone -- `serve` is a
    // separate HTTP/dashboard runtime, only needed by the imperative plugin
    // path we're not using. No local HTTP listener here means no reason to
    // set ENGRAM_HTTP_TOKEN either (that token authenticates one HTTP admin
    // route on `serve`, unrelated to cloud -- moot when `serve` never runs).
    //
    // Absolute path, not bare "engram": this repo deliberately keeps
    // Homebrew off PATH by default (see .profile.homebrew) so bare "engram"
    // would not resolve inside OpenCode's own process environment.
    //
    // Scope of "local-only": the memory STORE never syncs or leaves this
    // machine (no ENGRAM_CLOUD_*/autosync vars set, anywhere -- Cloud and
    // Git-Sync are both opt-in features this config never enables). Recalled
    // memory CONTENT is not further restricted -- a Siemens/Azure/Claude
    // session can still see memories in its context the same as any other
    // file you'd let that session read today. If stricter content isolation
    // for the Ollama "local" preset's sessions is wanted later, that needs
    // explicit scoping (e.g. a project/scope split), not assumed from this
    // block alone.
    //
    // Concurrency: Engram's SQLite layer uses WAL mode + 5000ms busy_timeout
    // + synchronous NORMAL (confirmed in its own DOCS.md) -- designed for
    // multiple concurrent process writers. Still worth a live verification
    // once deployed: open sessions in 2-3 harnesses against the same
    // project, write a distinct memory from each, confirm nothing is lost
    // (`engram doctor` after).
    "engram": {
      "type": "local",
      "command": [
        "{env:HOME}/PACKAGEMGMT/Homebrew/bin/engram",
        "mcp",
        "--tools=agent"
      ],
      "enabled": true
    }
  }
}
